Z||000000||ur2biteme||01-07-2001||03:38 AM||||i was wondering, what was the compression on the duke (87 preferably) when it rolled off the line.  not the ratio, but the p.s.i.  when i checked mine at 140k, it averaged at 147, with the high at 151, low at 142.  just wanted to know how close to factory spec i was.  thanks<P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||01-07-2001||09:40 AM||||If you blocked the throttle open and brought the engine to operating temperature than 150psi is very good. I got 150 psi with a 91 Duke that had 20,000 miles on it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||01-07-2001||03:57 PM||||The manual left Compression Pressure blank, but says 100 PSI minimum and no one cylinder less than 70% of the highest.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ur2biteme||01-07-2001||06:38 PM||||the reason why i  ask is because it's gonna be coming out of the shop soon, and i was checking if i should be worried about rebuilding it soon.  the compression shouldnt have lowered much since i checked it last, and i'm thinking about putting metal timing gears in as preventative maintinence.  <P>the idle is kinda lopy at best, but i still cant figure out wtf it is.  <P>if i remember right, the cylinders read out 1-4: 151, 147, 143, 142.  i'm thinking that if they read out like that at 140k, then it shouldnt be much different at a decently maintained 170k.  i dont want to have to tear the engine apart if i dont have to, especially since i plan on going for a quad 4 in a year or so anyway.  <P>thanks for the input||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||01-07-2001||06:53 PM||||Well, considering the lowest is 94% of the highest, I'd say you are in good shape barring any big failure. 150 PSI is good.||||reg||14
